7592186431443 1077 "selection" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431443 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431389 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431443 1079 #uuid "ee49f43f-642d-52e4-92e0-629fb560b5c7" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431438 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431444 1069 "q-3985" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431444 1071 "Solve for $t$ where $-2{t}^{3}-10{t}^{2}+10t=-14t-2{t}^{2}.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431444 1072 #uuid "2ace9a7c-7f26-5739-81ff-450b62fa7683" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431444 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(t)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{t}^{3}-10{t}^{2}+10t & =-14t-2{t}^{2} \\\\ -2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+10t & =-14t \\\\ -2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+24t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $-2t.$ We can factor out the $-2t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+24t & =0 \\\\ -2t({t}^{2}+4t-12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${t}^{2}+4t-12$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t({t}^{2}+4t-12) & =0 \\\\ -2t(t+6)(t-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0,$ $t=-6,$ and $t=2.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431437 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431445 1069 "q-37615" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431445 1071 "Find the solutions of the equation $20{p}^{3}+7{p}^{2}=6p.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431445 1072 #uuid "3a08949f-c53d-51fb-8ad8-329357cd31bd" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431445 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(p)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{p}^{3}+7{p}^{2} & =6p \\\\ 20{p}^{3}+7{p}^{2}-6p & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $p.$ We can factor out the $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{p}^{3}+7{p}^{2}-6p & =0 \\\\ p(20{p}^{2}+7p-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $20{p}^{2}+7p-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(20{p}^{2}+7p-6) & =0 \\\\ p(5p-2)(4p+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $p=0,$ $p=\\frac{2}{5},$ and $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431435 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431446 1069 "q-3981" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431446 1071 "If $15{r}^{3}-50{r}^{2}=-40r$, then $r=$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431446 1072 #uuid "589b942b-2afa-544c-b0d4-95c41762ad42" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431446 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(r)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{r}^{3}-50{r}^{2} & =-40r \\\\ 15{r}^{3}-50{r}^{2}+40r & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5r.$ We can factor out the $5r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{r}^{3}-50{r}^{2}+40r & =0 \\\\ 5r(3{r}^{2}-10r+8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $3{r}^{2}-10r+8$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5r(3{r}^{2}-10r+8) & =0 \\\\ 5r(3r-4)(r-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $r=0,$ $r=\\frac{4}{3},$ and $r=2.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431434 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431447 1069 "q-114097" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431447 1071 "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(w)=8{w}^{3}-20{w}^{2}-12w.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431447 1072 #uuid "598f0d12-0057-595a-88df-823e76f024dc" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431447 1074 "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(w)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n8{w}^{3}-20{w}^{2}-12w=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $4w.$ We can factor out the $4w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w(2{w}^{2}-5w-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{w}^{2}-5w-3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w(2{w}^{2}-5w-3) & =0 \\\\ 4w(2w+1)(w-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $w=0,$ $w=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $w=3.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431433 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431448 1069 "q-70389" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431448 1071 "Solve for $t$ if $4{t}^{3}-20{t}^{2}+24t=0.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431448 1072 #uuid "6a86a1c0-6162-5d37-ac2b-51fa127af01b" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431448 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $4t.$ We can factor out the $4t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{3}-20{t}^{2}+24t & =0 \\\\ 4t({t}^{2}-5t+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${t}^{2}-5t+6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t({t}^{2}-5t+6) & =0 \\\\ 4t(t-2)(t-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0$, $t=2$ and $t=3.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431442 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431449 1069 "q-70388" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431449 1071 "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(y)=10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-5y.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431449 1072 #uuid "6dba95b8-77df-52c1-b86b-bcff843030e4" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431449 1074 "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(y)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-5y=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5y.$ We can factor out the $5y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y(2{y}^{2}+y-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{y}^{2}+y-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y(2{y}^{2}+y-1) & =0 \\\\ 5y(2y-1)(y+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $y=0,$ $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $y=-1$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431439 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431450 1069 "q-114108" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431450 1071 "If $14{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2}=2{p}^{4}+17{p}^{2},$ then what are the values of $p?$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431450 1072 #uuid "a572c317-dda1-5da3-ae6b-b774d6ba6280" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431450 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2} & =2{p}^{4}+17{p}^{2} \\\\ 12{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2} & =17{p}^{2} \\\\ 12{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}-12{p}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2{p}^{2}.$ We can factor out the $2{p}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}-12{p}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}(6{p}^{2}-5p-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $6{p}^{2}-5p-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{p}^{2}(6{p}^{2}-5p-6) & =0 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}(3p+2)(2p-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $p=0,$ $p=-\\frac{2}{3},$ and $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431440 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431451 1069 "q-14007" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431451 1071 "Determine the real roots of the cubic equation $12{s}^{3}+12{s}^{2}-20s=6{s}^{2}+16s.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431451 1072 #uuid "ae27ee23-00a1-5698-9779-e950f4da30dd" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431451 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(s)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{s}^{3}+12{s}^{2}-20s & =6{s}^{2}+16s \\\\ 12{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}-20s & =16s \\\\ 12{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}-36s & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $6s.$ We can factor out the $6s$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}-36s & =0 \\\\ 6s(2{s}^{2}+s-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{s}^{2}+s-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6s(2{s}^{2}+s-6) & =0 \\\\ 6s(2s-3)(s+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $s=0,$ $s=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $s=-2.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431441 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431452 1069 "q-114116" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431452 1071 "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(w)=60{w}^{6}-105{w}^{5}+45{w}^{4}.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431452 1072 #uuid "af7e750a-c93b-50d4-aa96-76ae41484ef4" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431452 1074 "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(w)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n60{w}^{6}-105{w}^{5}+45{w}^{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $15{w}^{4}.$ We can factor out the $15{w}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{w}^{4}(4{w}^{2}-7w+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $4{w}^{2}-7w+3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{w}^{4}(4{w}^{2}-7w+3) & =0 \\\\ 15{w}^{4}(4w-3)(w-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $w=0,$ $w=\\frac{3}{4},$ and $w=1.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431436 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431453 1069 "q-71776" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431453 1071 "Solve the equation $2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}+24x=0.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431453 1072 #uuid "b9f18fac-7576-5250-96fc-957f32166255" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431453 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x.$ We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}+24x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}+7x+12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${x}^{2}+7x+12$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x({x}^{2}+7x+12) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=-4,$ and $x=-3.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431443 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431454 1069 "q-70467" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431454 1071 "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(z)=18{z}^{4}+3{z}^{3}-3{z}^{2}.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431454 1072 #uuid "d93d4254-999a-5b0a-8dfe-92904ca8400e" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true] [17592186431454 1074 "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(z)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n18{z}^{4}+3{z}^{3}-3{z}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3{z}^{2}.$ We can factor out the $3{z}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}(6{z}^{2}+z-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $6{z}^{2}+z-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}(6{z}^{2}+z-1) & =0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}(3z-1)(2z+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139534660 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-01025723-2564-5015-9d6f-9159e6f39f1f" 17592186431358 "answer-0346dd7f-1ea8-565f-ae3a-2489bdd2b183" 17592186431359 "answer-03d8b461-4ae9-52a8-aca8-51e298ef0397" 17592186431360 "answer-08e2c11b-95eb-5f08-a9a0-8be6291b080e" 17592186431361 "answer-09e9fedc-4d5e-5788-be94-10e0f727eb89" 17592186431362 "answer-0a49e338-c372-5407-9df4-fad1f40d4c65" 17592186431363 "answer-0b30b2b8-aa8f-5c31-b7e3-bc987c2700fb" 17592186431364 "answer-14dc1c65-04df-5fbd-be61-4477e30ea100" 17592186431365 "answer-17dca348-b860-5d6a-8c49-01c5dabaa699" 17592186431366 "answer-1b1433db-adc1-52e3-b005-6ae48814f3e5" 17592186431367 "answer-1b6656eb-e900-52d1-80d6-743f4beaedf4" 17592186431368 "answer-21aebf18-ce11-5430-a764-9bb724cacfd6" 17592186431369 "answer-29b0aa04-be1e-54a7-b230-b4434a74a514" 17592186431370 "answer-2b89dd01-b3fc-5526-9c13-3a06aab8a233" 17592186431371 "answer-2c17f057-bfa1-5976-b130-cd6d0a8fb8f9" 17592186431372 "answer-2cca6df7-9375-5547-918c-7f576ca1fd47" 17592186431373 "answer-2d